{
  "diagnosis": "The SQL query contains a syntax error in the recursive CTE definition where a second SELECT statement is incorrectly appended after the WHERE clause of the recursive part.",
  "root_cause": "The SQL statement 'SELECT n + 1 FROM seq WHERE n < 5 SELECT n FROM seq;' attempts to execute two SELECT statements in a single recursive UNION ALL clause, which is syntactically invalid. The second 'SELECT n FROM seq;' is not part of a valid UNION ALL operation and causes the parser to fail.",
  "corrected_sql": "WITH RECURSIVE seq(n) AS (VALUES (1) UNION ALL SELECT n + 1 FROM seq WHERE n < 5) SELECT n FROM seq;",
  "explanation": "The original SQL incorrectly places 'SELECT n FROM seq;' immediately after the WHERE clause of the recursive SELECT, creating malformed syntax. The recursive CTE should consist of a base case (VALUES (1)) followed by a UNION ALL and a recursive case (SELECT n + 1 FROM seq WHERE n < 5). The outer SELECT 'SELECT n FROM seq;' is correctly placed after the CTE definition. Removing the erroneous second SELECT fixes the syntax error.",
  "dialect_compatibility": {
    "status": "compatible",
    "explanation": "The corrected SQL follows standard PostgreSQL recursive CTE syntax which is compatible with PostgreSQL 14.24."
  },
  "version_compatibility": {
    "status": "compatible",
    "explanation": "The corrected SQL uses standard recursive CTE syntax supported in PostgreSQL 14.24, as confirmed by the official documentation."
